    "duplicated_columns_finding": "The source laboratory export contains duplicated COLUMNS: `last_hgb`, `last_plt`, `last_lymphocytes` and `last_neutrophils` hold byte-identical values under four different analyte names, and the four matching `first_*` columns do the same. Four distinct analytes cannot legitimately agree to the digit on every row, so this is a defect in the export, not a coincidence. It was detected by comparing columns directly after the audit flagged that they shared identical missingness AND identical unique-value counts. Impact on this project: none. All eight columns are 96-99% missing and were removed by the feature-selection missingness filter long before modelling, so no corrupted value reaches the deployed model. It is reported because a future version that tried to use `first_*` or `last_*` laboratory features would silently train on four copies of one unidentified analyte.",
    "action_taken": "The 40.3% exact duplicates in the prior-medication file are the same drug administered more than once on the same day; exposure is counted in distinct drug-days, so they were collapsed rather than discarded. In the cohort, every duplicated (order, organism, antibiotic) key carried CONFLICTING results \u2014 one specimen yielding two strains of the same species with different susceptibility. These were resolved by most-resistant-wins, the conservative clinical reading. The analytic table now contains zero duplicate keys, asserted by `12_TESTS/test_data_integrity.py`."
  },
